WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.760
This is a pie chart.

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:05.400
And this is Scottish political
economist William Playfair,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.800
who created the world's
first pie chart,

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.840
along with some of the first
line and bar charts.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:14.160
Data visualisation
is everywhere today,

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.320
but when Playfair first created them
over 200 years ago,

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.280
using shapes to represent numbers
was largely sneered at.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:26.920
How could drawings truly
represent solid scientific data?

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.200
But now data visualisation
has become an art form of its own.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.800
Here are five charts that have,

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.560
in their own way, changed the world.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.200
One of the best known uses
of data visualisation

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:45.760
was devised in London in 1854.

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.440
At the time, it was widely believed

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.480
that infectious diseases like
cholera were spread through the air.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.120
Surgeon John Snow felt differently.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:58.880
He was convinced that infections
could be spread by dirty water.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.320
And when a deadly outbreak
of cholera occurred in Soho,

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.360
Snow tracked each case and
marked them with a dot on a map.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.160
A clear picture emerged.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.640
All the deaths from cholera

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.320
were clustered around a water pump
on Broad Street.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:17.200
When he presented his findings
to the local officials,

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.840
the pump was shut down
and the outbreak ended.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.680
Dr John Snow fundamentally changed
our understanding of microbes,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.680
and his simple dot maps
were a huge part of that.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.920
Around about the same time,

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.640
"the lady with the lamp",
Florence Nightingale,

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.960
used a cunning visual
to save even more lives.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:41.800
During the Crimean War,

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:44.600
she realised that unsanitary
conditions in hospitals

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:48.520
were killing more soldiers than were
dying on the battlefield itself.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.440
She wanted to persuade
Queen Victoria,

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.640
head of the British Army
at the time,

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.680
to fund better conditions
in military hospitals.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.760
So she created the Coxcomb,

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.720
a sort of uneven pie chart

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.320
which showcased the data

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.720
on the causes of soldiers' deaths
in a highly effective way.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.720
Her efforts had sweeping impact,

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:10.240
not only improving
the sanitary conditions

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:11.760
of army hospitals at the time,

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:15.240
but ultimately changing
the face of modern healthcare.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.960
One of the most original examples
of data visualisation

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.440
was created by American historian

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.240
and civil rights activist
W.E.B Du Bois

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.640
for the 1900 Paris World's Fair.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.000
He presented a series
of hand-made charts

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:36.760
showcasing the educational,
social and business accomplishments

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.240
of black Americans in the 35 years

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.240
since slavery had been
officially abolished.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.240
The materials both challenged
stereotypes

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.640
and illustrated
how black communities

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.720
were still being discriminated
against.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:51.680
Du Bois hoped the visualisations

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.880
would help to end racial prejudice
in the world.

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.920
"The problem of the 20th century

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.000
"is the problem of the color-line,"
he wrote.

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:03.560
Although his dreams weren't
realised, Du Bois' beautiful charts

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.280
are now recognised as
a powerful use of data visualisation

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.480
to demonstrate social change.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:16.520
Data visualisation can also
be used for misinformation,

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.960
sometimes
with disastrous consequences.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:22.640
This is the Kallikak family tree.

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.600
As a chart,
it's not ground-breaking,

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.720
but its impact was profound.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.920
It was conjured up by Henry Goddard,

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:32.480
an American psychologist
and eugenicist

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.640
who believed that one
of society's greatest problems

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:37.400
was "feeble-minded" people -

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.440
basically, anyone deemed
to be of lower intelligence.

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:44.720
Goddard wrote a book based
on the story of Martin Kallikak,

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:47.480
a soldier who was married
and had a family

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.680
of fine, upstanding citizens.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:51.440
But according to the book,

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.680
Martin Kallikak
had a one-night stand

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.000
with a "feeble-minded" barmaid.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:59.000
She had a son who became
known as "Old Horror",

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.680
who went on to father ten children.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:05.400
According to Goddard,
feeble-mindedness was hereditary,

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.640
and the family was full
of what he called

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.640
"the lowest types of human beings".

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.080
But it was a fiction.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.480
The barmaid never existed.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:18.600
Even so, the Kallikak family tree
could be found in school textbooks

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.760
as late as the 1950s, and it was
also used by the eugenics movement

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.000
for decades afterwards.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.400
And it was adopted by the Nazis,
who used it in propaganda films

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:32.520
to gain support
for their racial purity laws,

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.080
which led to the murder of thousands
of disabled people in the Holocaust.

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.880
Sometimes, iconic status
can be achieved almost accidentally.

00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:47.480
In 2018, scientist Ed Hawkins

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.440
was invited to give a speech
about climate change at Hay,

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.520
a literary festival in Wales.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.960
He needed to convey the increase
in global average temperatures

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.640
to a largely
non-scientific audience.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:04.000
So, he devised a chart
that used no words, just colours.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:06.200
It was a stunningly simple way

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.920
of demonstrating how the planet
had warmed since 1850.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:13.520
The chart, known
as the warming stripes,

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.840
uses blues to indicate
cooler than average years

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:20.880
and reds to show years with
temperatures hotter than average.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.440
Updated every year,

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:26.560
Hawkins says 2022 was the first year

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.800
that every country
needed a new red stripe,

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:33.920
demonstrating how fast
the Earth's climate is changing.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:37.800
Although the graphic breaks some
of the rules of data visualisation -

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.000
it doesn't provide
a title or legend -

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:45.040
arguably, it is one of the most
iconic graphics of modern times.

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:48.880
What do you think the next chart
to change the world will be?

